<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dreyfus Affair</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1894">1894</a>–<a href="/years/1906">1906</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dreyfus Affair was a political scandal that divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1894 to 1906. Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-dreyfus">Alfred Dreyfus</a>, a Jewish army officer, was wrongly convicted of spying for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and the army covered up evidence pointing to Major Ferdinand Esterhazy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emile-zola">Émile Zola</a>'s open letter J'Accuse in 1898 turned it into a national conflict between republicans and their nationalist, Catholic and antisemitic opponents, and Dreyfus was finally cleared in 1906.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dreyfus-affair.jpg" alt="Dreyfus Affair" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aron Gerschel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfred_Dreyfus_(1859-1935)_-_photo_originale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1894 the Third Republic was twenty-four years old and its ministries were short-lived. The annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a> fed an extreme nationalism, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">antisemitism</a> had grown virulent since the publication of Jewish France by Édouard Drumont in 1886. In September 1894 a housekeeper placed in the German Embassy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> by the Military Intelligence Service found a torn-up note, later called the bordereau, addressed to the German military attaché Maximilian von Schwartzkoppen, offering confidential French military documents. The Minister of War, General Auguste Mercier, ordered an investigation that restricted the search to artillery officers on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a>, and Major du Paty de Clam found Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-dreyfus">Alfred Dreyfus</a> the probable author.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Dreyfus was arrested on 13 October 1894. On 22 December seven judges unanimously convicted him of collusion with a foreign power, and he was degraded and sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devils-island">Devil's Island</a>. His brother Mathieu worked for years to win a retrial. In March 1896 Lieutenant Colonel Georges Picquart found the petit bleu, an unsent telegram addressed to Major Ferdinand Walsin Esterhazy. Picquart's superiors ignored him and sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, and in January 1898 a court martial acquitted Esterhazy.</p><p>On 13 January 1898 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emile-zola">Émile Zola</a> published J'Accuse...! in L'Aurore. He was tried for defamation, and antisemitic riots broke out in cities across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Dreyfus returned to France in 1899 and was tried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennes">Rennes</a>, where on 9 September the court convicted him again, by five votes to two, with extenuating circumstances, and sentenced him to ten years. President Émile Loubet pardoned him on 19 September 1899.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The pardon did not declare Dreyfus innocent, and his rehabilitation was completed only in 1906, when passions had cooled. Reinstated in the army as a major, he served throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and ended his service as a lieutenant colonel. He died in 1935.</p><p>The affair split <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> between the Dreyfusards, among them Anatole France, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-clemenceau">Georges Clemenceau</a> and Sarah Bernhardt, and the anti-Dreyfusards, who were mostly pro-army and Catholic. Most historians hold that the conflict served the republican order, strengthening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary democracy</a> and defeating monarchist and reactionary forces. It also spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">antisemitism</a> through all strata of society. The journalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodor-herzl">Theodor Herzl</a>, who was present at Dreyfus's degradation in 1895, is often seen as moved by the affair toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zionism">Zionism</a>, though some scholars think he exaggerated its influence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dreyfus affair” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Dreyfus Affair</b> in France began in 1894, and Karl Lueger in Vienna and Adolf Stoecker in Germany used antisemitism in politics.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/antisemitism">Antisemitism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>After defeat by Germany in 1871 the return of Alsace-Lorraine drove French revanchism for a quarter century, and in the <b>Dreyfus Affair</b> of 1894–1906 conservative Catholic nationalists treated a Jewish officer as no true Frenchman.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>His innocence, and the cover-up, split France in the <b>Dreyfus Affair</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-22">22 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
